  "narrative": {
    "essence": "Brazil hasn't won the World Cup since 2002 — their longest drought since they first appeared in the tournament. The ache is national, persistent, and politically charged. In 2026, with Vinícius Jr. at peak powers and a rebuilt squad, they believe the hexacampeonato is finally within reach.",
    "footballIdentity": "Brazil and football are synonymous. Pelé, Ronaldo, Ronaldinho — the Seleção didn't just win World Cups, they defined what football could be at its most joyous. Jogo bonito isn't a tactic, it's a national value. But since 2002, that joy has curdled into anxiety. The 2014 humiliation — 7-1 to Germany at home — still echoes. Every Brazilian squad carries that ghost into every tournament.",
    "theMoment": "Brazil enters 2026 in political and social turbulence. Lula's government faces deep polarization, and football has been dragged into the culture wars — Vinícius Jr., Brazil's biggest star, has been a lightning rod for racism in European leagues and a defender of progressive causes at home. Whether this squad can unite rather than divide a fractured country is as interesting as whether they can win. The hexacampeonato would be the binding agent Brazil's political class can't manufacture.",
    "momentType": "footballing"
  },
